  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Find the smallest natural number with sixteen factors.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$120$.\n\n$120=2^3\\times3\\times5$ has $(3+1)(1+1)(1+1)=16$ factors.",
      "worked": "For $n=p^a q^b\\cdots$, each factor is formed by choosing an exponent from $0$ to the corresponding exponent in $n$. Thus the number of factors is $(a+1)(b+1)\\cdots$.\n\nThe exponent patterns giving sixteen factors are $(15)$, $(7,1)$, $(3,3)$, $(3,1,1)$ and $(1,1,1,1)$. For each pattern, use the smallest primes and assign the largest exponent to the smallest prime. The smallest candidates are:\n\n$$2^{15}=32768,\\qquad 2^7\\times3=384,$$\n$$2^3\\times3^3=216,\\qquad 2^3\\times3\\times5=120,$$\n$$2\\times3\\times5\\times7=210.$$\n\nThe least is $120$, and it has $(3+1)(1+1)(1+1)=16$ factors.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 76
  },
